\details{
Searches NCBI nucleotide by taxon name and barcode marker, retrieves full
taxonomy via the NCBI taxonomy database, filters by sequence length and
quality, optionally downsamples, and returns a \code{reference_df} ready for
\code{\link[=build_sequence_matrix]{build_sequence_matrix()}}.

The function performs a \strong{count-first estimation} before downloading.
If the total exceeds \code{max_sequences}, sequences are subsampled
proportionally across taxa (each taxon gets at least \code{min_per_taxon}
sequences). This ensures all taxa are represented while staying within
the download budget.
}
\section{Why not use match object accessions}{

The match object from TaxaMatch contains only sequences that happened to
match your queries -- a biased subset.
A good likelihood model needs the broader landscape: within-species
variation, between-species distances, and coverage of related taxa.
This function searches by \strong{taxon + marker} to get that full picture.
}

\section{Caching and resumability}{

For large searches (many taxa, slow NCBI responses), set \code{cache_dir} to
a directory path.
Completed per-taxon results are saved as \code{.rds} files.
If the function is interrupted, re-running with the same \code{cache_dir}
skips already-downloaded taxa.
}

